Fix issue for minikube start 
minikube delete 
docker ps -a 
docker rm -f minikube 
minikube start --driver=docker --cpus=2 --memory=4096


Load your Docker image into Minikube

Your image is local:

productapi:v1

Run:

minikube image load productapi:v1

Verify:

minikube image ls 
========================
STEP 1 — Create Deployment

Make sure your deployment.yaml has:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: productapi-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: productapi
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: productapi
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: productapi
        image: productapi:v1
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080

✅ STEP 2 — Apply Deployment
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

✅ STEP 3 — Create Service

service.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: productapi-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: productapi
  ports:
    - port: 8080
      targetPort: 8080
      nodePort: 30080

Apply:

kubectl apply -f service.yaml
🚀 STEP 4 — Check pods
kubectl get pods

Expected:

productapi-deployment-xxxxx   Running
🌐 STEP 5 — Access your API
minikube service productapi-service

OR manually:

http://localhost:30080

Swagger:

http://localhost:30080/swagger

----------------------------

minikube addons enable ingress
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx 

STEP 1 — Enable Ingress Controller

Run:

minikube addons enable ingress

Check:

kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx

You should see something like:

ingress-nginx-controller
🚀 STEP 2 — Keep your Service (NO CHANGE)

Your service stays:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: productapi-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: productapi
  ports:
    - port: 8080
      targetPort: 8080

👉 IMPORTANT: Ingress routes to this service

🚀 STEP 3 — Create Ingress YAML

Create:

📄 ingress.yaml

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: productapi-ingress
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
  rules:
  - host: productapi.local
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: productapi-service
            port:
              number: 8080
🚀 STEP 4 — Apply Ingress
kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml
🚀 STEP 5 — Get Minikube IP
minikube ip

You already have:

192.168.49.2
🚀 STEP 6 — Map domain in Windows HOSTS file

Open as ADMIN:

C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts

Add this line:

192.168.49.2   productapi.local
🚀 STEP 7 — Test in browser

Now open:
=======================================
http://productapi.local/swagger

Kubernetes  URL
http://127.0.0.1:8080/swagger/index.html
>net Core Docker URL 
http://localhost:8080/swagger/index.html  

===============================
Step 10: Deploy DEV
helm install productapi-dev ./k8s/helm -f ./k8s/helm/values-dev.yaml
Step 11: Deploy UAT
helm install productapi-uat ./k8s/helm -f ./k8s/helm/values-uat.yaml
Step 12: Deploy PROD
helm install productapi-prod ./k8s/helm -f ./k8s/helm/values-prod.yaml
Harness Integration

This is likely what you remember.

Harness has an environment variable:

ENVIRONMENT=dev

or

ENVIRONMENT=uat

or

ENVIRONMENT=prod

Then Harness executes:

helm upgrade --install productapi \
  ./k8s/helm \
  -f ./k8s/helm/values-${ENVIRONMENT}.yaml

Examples:

helm upgrade --install productapi ./k8s/helm -f values-dev.yaml
helm upgrade --install productapi ./k8s/helm -f values-uat.yaml
helm upgrade --install productapi ./k8s/helm -f values-prod.yaml